{
  "markdown_report": "At 10% progress, prioritize **agreement-weighted recent-history residuals with uniform multi-negative cross-entropy**. This tests a small extension before adding temporal or graph machinery; no improvement has been measured.\n\n**Insights and directions.** The starting proposal yields three insights. A zero-initialized gate preserves initial SASRec scores. It permits gradual use of additional history evidence. Five-item pooling emphasizes current activity. Unconditional pooling can nevertheless amplify an isolated purchase. Multi-negative cross-entropy introduces candidate competition. Its contribution must be separated from representation changes.\n\nRelated work suggests three complementary insights. CLSR separates persistent and immediate interests using history-derived supervision [1]. Agreement weighting offers a smaller hypothesis to test. gSASRec identifies sampled-BCE overconfidence as an objective concern [2]. Electronics’ 9.61% negative diagnostic margins motivate an objective ablation but do not identify its cause. Directed graph projections model chronological associations [3]. These associations cannot establish equipment compatibility or satisfied needs.\n\n| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intent representation | SASRec; CLSR | Agreement-weighted recent residual |\n| Ranking objective | Sampled BCE; gSASRec | Uniform multi-negative cross-entropy |\n| Behavioral direction | Directed transitions | Separate source/target score |\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each entry gives a positive and negative reason.\n\n- **A: Agreement residual + cross-entropy [1,2]: 5/7/7/7/4.** Originality: adaptive combination / familiar components; potential: reusable minimal branch / limited expressiveness; user: targets stale and isolated interests / cannot infer necessity; model: tests weaker separation / may amplify recency; difficulty: small extension / causal masking needs care.\n- **B: Cross-entropy alone [2]: 2/6/3/8/2.** Originality: clean control / established objective; potential: transferable / bounded contribution; user: sharper competition / no explicit intent mechanism; model: targets separation / diagnosis is in-sample; difficulty: simple loss / exclusions require care.\n- **C: Directed-transition residual [3]: 5/7/6/6/5.** Originality: asymmetric fusion / known transitions; potential: task sequences / sparse counts; user: captures successive needs / compatibility unverified; model: alternative separation / popularity confounds; difficulty: lightweight scoring / additional artifacts.\n\n**Selected algorithm.** A explicitly targets older/occasional interests outweighing current activity and broad associations displacing specific matches. Agreement with the latest action favors a coherent recent pattern; this is a predictive hypothesis, not proof of user intent. Cross-entropy addresses Electronics’ observed separation weakness.\n\n```text\nFor each valid causal prefix t:\n  h = SASRec(prefix)\n  For j among its last five items:\n    w[j] ∝ exp(-age[j] + cosine(e[j], e[t]))\n  r = LayerNorm(W * sum(w[j] * e[j]))\n  z = h + tanh(a) * r; initialize a = 0\n  Train CE over positive and 32 uniform distinct negatives\n```\n\nExclude padding and known training-history positives from negatives. Compare baseline, objective-only, residual-only, and combined variants under matched budgets. Select solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. Preserve four-category routing, seeds, data path, diagnostic interfaces, and predictor reconstruction. Pairwise diagnostics remain distinct from HR@10; full-catalog usefulness remains unproven.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add an agreement-weighted causal recent-history residual to category-specific SASRec, trained with uniform multi-negative sampled-candidate cross-entropy. Within the last five input interactions, exponential position decay and cosine agreement with the latest input item determine pooling weights. A projected, normalized residual enters through a zero-initialized scalar gate.",
    "motivation": "The selected idea addresses two user-facing failures: older or occasional interests receiving excessive influence, and broad associations competing with specific current-interest matches. Recent pooling limits the first mechanism, while agreement weighting tests whether coherent recent activity improves specificity. The objective separately targets Electronics’ relatively weak in-sample positive-versus-negative separation: 9.61% negative margins and a p10 margin near 0.054. These observations motivate experiments without establishing causes or expected gains. The method cannot infer equipment compatibility, ownership sufficiency, or purchase necessity.",
    "implementation_notes": "Use only interaction IDs for this initial experiment; no unavailable attributes or pretrained weights are needed. Compute every pool causally over nonpadding input positions, including the current input item but excluding its next-item target. Use position age, not elapsed time, initially. Initialize the residual projection normally and only the scalar gate to zero so the gate can receive a gradient. Preserve the original feature scale by adding branch-normalized residuals without normalizing the combined output. Sample up to 32 distinct uniform nonpadding negatives excluding the target and the user's known training positives, with a defined fallback when fewer are eligible. This is sampled-candidate cross-entropy, without claiming an unbiased full-softmax estimator. Retain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, SASREC_EPOCHS environment default 200, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], original splits, and all four category routes. Save configurations and checkpoints beneath each trial directory and update the predictor writer to reconstruct the residual architecture. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), including measured metrics and definitions. Keep view.model bound to the trained module and log2feats returning the combined features. Compare baseline, objective-only, residual-only, and combined variants within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget; select using independently measured macro validation HR@10. Report pairwise margins and perturbations only within their stated in-sample scope. No experiments were executed for this report.",
    "pseudocode": "initialize category-specific SASRec, projection W, residual LayerNorm, scalar a=0\nfor sequence, positive, negative in training batches:\n    h = original_SASRec_features(sequence)\n    e = item_emb(sequence)\n    for each nonpadding position t:\n        J = nonpadding positions from max(0,t-4) through t\n        logits_j = -(t-j) + cosine(e[j], e[t])\n        weights = softmax(logits_j over J)\n        r[t] = LayerNorm(W(sum_j weights[j] * e[j]))\n    z = h + tanh(a) * r\n    candidates = positive plus up to 32 distinct uniform eligible negatives per target\n    loss = mean cross_entropy(dot(z[t], item_emb(candidates[t])), target_index=0)\n    update parameters using only valid target positions\nsave architecture configuration and state; regenerate compatible routed predictor\ncompare ablations by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Adds a small, explicit agreement weighting to the supplied recent-history residual and separates its contribution from candidate competition.",
      "negative": "Recency pooling, similarity weighting, residual gates, and sampled cross-entropy are established components; novelty lies primarily in their controlled combination."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Provides an inexpensive basis for later tests of multiple timescales, timestamp gaps, or persistent-interest agreement.",
      "negative": "Agreement may reinforce broad similarity or an anomalous latest purchase, and validation gains would not establish improved full-catalog usefulness."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Requires one short causal pooling branch and a loss change while retaining SASRec's embedding and scoring interfaces.",
      "negative": "Correct padding, negative exclusions, gradient initialization, diagnostic compatibility, and predictor reconstruction require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "CLSR: Contrastive Learning for Long and Short-term Interest Modeling",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13090",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses history-derived long-term and short-term interest proxies with contrastive supervision and adaptive aggregation.",
        "Motivates distinguishing persistent preference from immediate activity."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Separate branches do not by themselves establish disentangled interests.",
        "The proposed agreement residual is a simpler adaptation, not a reproduction or demonstrated improvement."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "gSASRec (2024 extended abstract)",
      "link": "https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939",
      "contributions": [
        "Examines overconfidence associated with sampled binary cross-entropy in SASRec.",
        "Proposes generalized binary cross-entropy, motivating objective-focused controls."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that the supplied Electronics diagnostic weakness is caused by the training objective.",
        "Does not establish gains for the proposed cross-entropy variant on this pinned benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revisiting Graph Projections for Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.09209v1",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines normalized interaction graph projections with directed, inverse-distance-weighted purchase transitions.",
        "Motivates an asymmetric transition-score alternative."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its category-based regularization cannot be transferred directly because product categories are unavailable here.",
        "Chronological associations are proxies rather than verified complementarity or compatibility labels."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
